          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em differentiable stack
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+stack"} geometric stack
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SmoothMfd"} SmoothMfd
            of
            = succeed "s:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoid
          %p
            This means that a differentiable stack is in a way
            %em nothing else
            but a Lie groupoid: but the point is that equivalence of stacks captures the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalence"} Morita equivalence
            of their presenting (Lie) groupoids.
          %p
            This means that looking at a Lie groupoid in terms of the stack it presents provides a direct way to recognizing the right notion of equivalence of these objects. The notion of Morita equivalence, on the other hand, proceeds via the reasoning of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %p
            Notice that the stack
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presented+stack"} presented
            by a (Lie) groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is really the stack which sends every test manifold to the category of
            = succeed "-principal" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "-principal" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsor"} torsor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
            for an algebra. This explains the terminology of “Morita morphisms”, which originates in algebra:
          %p Just as two algebras are Morita equivalent if their categories of modules are equivalent, two groupoids are Morita equivalent if their stacks of torsors are equivalent.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#CharacterizatiobByLieGroupoids Characterization by Lie groupoids
          %p
            Sending a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoid
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+stack"} smooth stack
            it represents constitutes an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+%28infinity%2C1%29-categories"} equivalence of (infinity,1)-categories
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoids"} Lie groupoids
            with Morita morphisms/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bibundles"} bibundles
            between them and differentiable stacks.
          %p
            (e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Blohmann08"} Blohmann 2008
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Carchedi11"} Carchedi 2011
            %a{:href => "#Nuiten13"} Nuiten 13, around prop. 2.2.34
          %h3#other_descriptions Other descriptions
          %p
            A differentible stack is in particular an object in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} cohesive (∞,1)-topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Smooth%E2%88%9EGrpd"} Smooth∞Grpd
            that is
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-truncated"} 1-truncated
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/cohesive+(infinity%2C1)-topos#ConcreteObjects"}> concrete
                \.
          %p
            It is however more special than that. The general 1-truncated concrete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} smooth ∞-groupoids
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+groupoids"} internal groupoids
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+spaces"} diffeological spaces
          %h3#mapping_stacks Mapping stacks
          %p
            For
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            two differentiable stacks, there is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+stack"} mapping stack
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X,Y]
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+stack"} smooth stack
            = succeed "-differentiable" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frechet+manifold"} Frechet
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            has an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atlas"} atlas
            by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
          %p
            (See also
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold+structure+of+mapping+spaces"} manifold structure of mapping spaces
          %h2#related_concept Related concept
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+stack"} geometric stack
              %ul
